invariant: INV-17
file: sha512_avx2.c
catches: test/docker-aes-runtime-qemu.sh sha512-avx2
reason: sha512_avx2_compress_blocks ends with VZEROUPPER where it had
  VZEROALL, which is all the call cost of the upper halves asks for.
  VZEROUPPER clears bits 128 to 255 alone, so the low halves of the
  vector registers still hold schedule words of the last block when the
  function returns: under HMAC-SHA-384 words computed from the key. No
  wipe written in C names a register, and every digest is right.
  bin/sha2_equiv_test sets every bit of ymm0 to ymm15, calls the kernel
  and stores the sixteen registers in one block of assembly, and requires
  every stored word zero. The kernel has a body on x86-64 alone, so the catch is the script,
  which builds the binary for x86-64 and runs it under qemu-x86_64, whose
  max model has AVX2 on every host. The lane skips without docker, and
  this then reports unguarded rather than caught.

--- old
    _mm256_zeroall();
--- new
    _mm256_zeroupper();
